 "voice": [
  {
   "id": "vo01",
   "t": 0.1,
   "dur": 4.789,
   "file": "vo/01.wav",
   "text": "Why is this cell folded? Because the road ahead is narrower than the cell."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo02",
   "t": 5.9,
   "dur": 10.724,
   "file": "vo/02.wav",
   "text": "Meet a red blood cell: a dimpled disc, about seven micrometers across. It even pushed out its own nucleus. You have about twenty-five trillion of them: most of your own cells."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo03",
   "t": 17.5,
   "dur": 8.456,
   "file": "vo/03.wav",
   "text": "Follow it as the vessel branches, and branches again, into a capillary. There's no room to overtake, so cells go through in single file."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo04",
   "t": 26.7,
   "dur": 8.015,
   "file": "vo/04.wav",
   "text": "You'd guess it stretches to fit. It can't. The dimples give it spare surface for its volume, so it folds into a parachute instead."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo05",
   "t": 37.9,
   "dur": 8.817,
   "file": "vo/05.wav",
   "text": "The tightest are about three micrometers: less than half its width. And it keeps squeezing through, all its life: about a hundred and twenty days."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo06",
   "t": 50.7,
   "dur": 6.574,
   "file": "vo/06.wav",
   "text": "Stiff sickle cells can jam the smallest vessels, where oxygen is handed out. That dimple isn't decoration."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo07",
   "t": 58.3,
   "dur": 5.093,
   "file": "vo/07.wav",
   "text": "Meanwhile, as you watched, your bone marrow made over a hundred million new red cells."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo08",
   "t": 64.3,
   "dur": 4.14,
   "file": "vo/08.wav",
   "text": "Next: what this cell carries. Room for about a billion passengers."
  }
 ]
}